.TH BLINK 1 "September 2026" "acer-powersave" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
blink \- flash the keyboard zones, the way beep flashes the speaker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blink
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fB\-n\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]...]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Flashes the RGB zones of an Acer Predator or Nitro keyboard. The options
mirror
.BR beep (1):
.B \-l
is the length of a flash,
.B \-d
the gap between repeats,
.B \-r
how many times, and
.B \-n
starts another flash with its own options. What beep calls a frequency is a
colour here.
.PP
Unless
.B \-\-keep
is given, blink saves the keyboard state when it starts and restores it when
it exits, including an animated effect if one was running. The common case,
warning the user and leaving things as they were, therefore needs no shell
scripting around it.
.PP
It writes through the standard LED class devices published by the
linuwu_sense driver, so each zone keeps its own brightness:
.PP
.RS
/sys/class/leds/acer\-wmi:multicolor:kbd_zoned_backlight\-\fIzone\fR
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-color =\fICOLOUR\fR
Colour of the flash. A name or \fIRRGGBB\fR, with or without a leading hash.
Default red.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-color\-end =\fICOLOUR\fR
Fade from \fB\-\-color\fR to this one over the length of the flash.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-brightness =\fI0\-100\fR
Brightness of the flash. Default 100.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-brightness\-end =\fI0\-100\fR
Fade the brightness to this value over the flash.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zones =\fIBITS\fR
Which zones to light, as binary digits read left to right: \fB1000\fR is the
leftmost zone, \fB0001\fR the rightmost, \fB1111\fR all of them. Shorter
strings are padded on the right, so \fB1\fR means the first zone only.
Default \fB1111\fR.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length =\fIMS\fR
Length of each flash in milliseconds. Default 200.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delay =\fIMS\fR
Gap between repeats. Defaults to the same as \fB\-\-length\fR.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-repeat =\fIN\fR
How many times to flash. Default 1.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-new
Start another flash with its own options, as in beep.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Leave the last colour on instead of restoring the previous state.
.TP
.B \-\-restore\-only
Print the current state and exit without changing anything.
.SH EXAMPLES
Flash red twice on an error and put everything back:
.PP
.RS
.B blink \-c red \-r 2 \-l 150
.RE
.PP
Fade the rightmost zone from green to blue over a second:
.PP
.RS
.B blink \-z 0001 \-c green \-C blue \-l 1000
.RE
.PP
A two-tone alert, as beep would chain two notes:
.PP
.RS
.B blink \-c orange \-l 100 \-n \-c red \-l 300
.RE
.PP
Leave the keyboard dim white, without restoring:
.PP
.RS
.B blink \-c white \-b 20 \-l 0 \-\-keep
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, 1 if the LEDs cannot be found or written to.
.SH PERMISSIONS
Writing to the LEDs needs membership of the \fBlinuwu_sense\fR group, which a
udev rule shipped with this package grants on the two writable attributes:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo usermod \-aG linuwu_sense $USER
.RE
.PP
The change takes effect on your next login.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR beep (1),
.BR acer-powersave (1)
